<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hayreddin Barbarossa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1478–<a href="/years/1546">1546</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hayreddin Barbarossa was a sixteenth-century corsair who became grand admiral of the Ottoman fleet. He secured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a> for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> and won a major victory over a Christian fleet at Preveza in 1538. He helped make the Ottoman Empire the dominant naval power in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hayreddin-barbarossa.jpg" alt="Hayreddin Barbarossa" width="236" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: AsiaticLion7 · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hayreddin_-_Algeria_Fine_Art_Museum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Hayreddin was born on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesbos">Lesbos</a>, and was called Khizr or Hızır as a child. His father, Yakup Ağa, was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sipahi">sipahi</a> who had taken part in the Ottoman conquest of the island in 1462 and became an established potter. His mother, Katerina, was a Greek Orthodox woman from Lesbos. Of the four sons, Oruç, Ilyas and Khizr all became seamen. The brothers turned privateers against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-hospitaller">Knights Hospitaller</a> based on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a>. The Knights killed Ilyas in a fight, and Oruç rowed as a galley slave for the Order for four years until his father paid a ransom.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1503 Oruç made the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/djerba">Djerba</a> his base, and Khizr joined him. The brothers were later allowed to use the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-goulette">La Goulette</a>, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a>, on condition of giving one third of their spoils to its sultan. Between 1504 and 1510 Oruç carried Muslim Mudéjars from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>, and the title Baba Oruç, meaning Father Oruç, is said to have become Barbarossa in Spain, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>.</p><p>In 1516 the brothers took Jijel and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a> from the Spaniards. In 1517 Oruç offered Algiers to the Ottoman sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selim-i">Selim I</a>, who accepted it as a province. Oruç and his brother Ishak were killed in combat near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlemcen">Tlemcen</a> in 1518.</p><p>Khizr then offered his allegiance to Selim and in 1519 received the title of beylerbey, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janissaries">janissaries</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galley">galleys</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannons</a>. He defeated a Spanish and Italian army that tried to retake <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a> in 1519 and raided the coasts of Provence and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. In 1533 Sultan Suleiman appointed him <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kapudan-pasha">Kapudan Pasha</a>. In 1534 he sailed with 80 galleys, raided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calabria">Calabria</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-naples">Gulf of Naples</a>, and captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a>, forcing the Hafsid sultan Mulay Hassan to flee.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Barbarossa retired to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 1545, leaving his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasan-pasha-son-of-hayreddin">Hasan Pasha</a> in charge in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a>, and dictated his memoirs, five hand-written volumes known as the Gazavat-ı Hayreddin Paşa. He died in 1546 in his seaside palace at Büyükdere on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosphorus">Bosphorus</a>. He is buried in a mausoleum built in 1541 by the architect <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mimar-sinan">Mimar Sinan</a> near the ferry port of Beşiktaş, at the site where his fleet used to assemble. The Ottoman supremacy in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> that he established lasted until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lepanto">Battle of Lepanto</a> in 1571.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hayreddin Barbarossa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Spain took Bejaia and Tripoli in 1510, while the brothers Oruç and <b>Hayreddin Barbarossa</b> operated from La Goulette and Jerba with Hafsid permission before founding their own state at Algiers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hafsid-sultanate">Hafsid Sultanate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>…1521, the destruction of the Hungarian royal army at the Battle of Mohács in 1526, the first siege of Vienna in 1529, and the naval operations of <b>Hayreddin Barbarossa</b>, whose victory at Preveza in 1538 secured Ottoman naval dominance in the eastern and central Mediterranean for a generation.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>His brother <b>Hayreddin Barbarossa</b>, facing Spanish counter-attacks and local rivals, offered Algiers to the Ottoman Sultan Selim I around 1519.</blockquote><a href="/empire/regency-of-algiers">Regency of Algiers · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Barbarossa</b>'s raids of 1537 made the duchy an Ottoman tributary.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-the-archipelago">Duchy of the Archipelago</a></li><li><blockquote>Belkadi first allied with the <b>Barbarossa</b> brothers, then turned against Hayreddin, defeated him and held Algiers from about 1520 until he was killed around 1527.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-kuku">Kingdom of Kuku</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Mytilene, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=39.11,26.55&amp;year=1522" aria-label="Mytilene on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5207 762 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Hayreddin Barbarossa"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5657 1032h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5657 1032h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=39.11,26.55&amp;year=1522">What was here in 1522? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
